      "evidence_id": "e-fd4a37430d51f9429117",
      "text": "It’s 5.30pm on the tiny Greek island of Kimolos and its cobblestone streets are deserted. Late afternoon sun filters through peach bougainvillaea, and blue-and-white Greek flag bunting – suspended between family-run delicatessens and ceramics shops – sways gently in the breeze. Here, in the sleepy hilltop village of Chorio, the pace of life is snail-slow. Owners shut up shop in the early afternoon and don’t reopen until the evening, when locals reappear from sugarcube houses for a strong cup of coffee or ice-cold drink at traditional kafeneia.\nIt’s all in stark contrast to other Greek islands I’ve visited. If I were in Mykonos, Santorini or the up-and-coming Paros, the town would be heaving with tourists queuing for a sunset snap or TikTok-famous beachfront bar. But despite its Cycladic charms, Kimolos is one of the few destinations in the popular island group that remains largely undiscovered. Even its neighbour Milos, just a few kilometres across the sea, has seen a sharp surge in tourism in the last couple of years. But during my three days on Kimolos, reached via a new four-hour ferry route from Athens, I haven't met anyone who isn’t staycationing from the mainland.",
